$(1)$ If the solution set of this inequality is $\left\{x|-1 < x < - \dfrac{1}{2}\right\}$, find the value of the real number $a$;  
$(2)$ If $a \leqslant 0$, solve this inequality about $x$.

s:
Solution:  
$(1)$ Since the solution set of the inequality $(ax-1)(x+1) > 0$ is $\left\{x|-1 < x < - \dfrac{1}{2}\right\}$,  
the two roots of the equation $(ax-1)(x+1)=0$ are $-1$ and $- \dfrac{1}{2}$;  
Therefore, $- \dfrac{1}{2}a-1=0$,  
Thus, $a=-2$;  
$(2)$ Since $(ax-1)(x+1) > 0$,  
when $a < 0$, the inequality can be transformed into $(x- \dfrac{1}{a})(x+1) < 0$;  
If $a < -1$, then $\dfrac{1}{a} > -1$, and the solution is $-1 < x < \dfrac{1}{a}$;  
If $a=-1$, then $\dfrac{1}{a}=-1$, and the solution set is $\varnothing$;  
If $-1 < a < 0$, then $\dfrac{1}{a} < -1$, and the solution is $\dfrac{1}{a} < x < -1$;  
When $a=0$, the inequality becomes $-(x+1) > 0$, and the solution is $x < -1$;  
When $a > 0$, the inequality becomes $(x- \dfrac{1}{a})(x+1) > 0$,  
since $\dfrac{1}{a} > -1$, the solution of the inequality is $x < -1$ or $x > \dfrac{1}{a}$;  
In summary, when $a < -1$, the solution set of the inequality is $\boxed{\{x|-1 < x < \dfrac{1}{a}\}}$;  
When $a=-1$, the solution set of the inequality is $\boxed{\varnothing}$;  
When $-1 < a < 0$, the solution set of the inequality is $\boxed{\{x| \dfrac{1}{a} < x < -1\}}$;  
When $a=0$, the solution set of the inequality is $\boxed{\{x|x < -1\}}$;  
When $a > 0$, the solution set of the inequality is $\boxed{\{x|x < -1,或x > \dfrac{1}{a}\}}$.

